<Layout
  title="Introducing gate scan: know your PII exposure before the AI does — gate blog"
  description="gate scan reads your database schema and classifies every column by PII tier — so you can configure redaction before you ever connect an AI agent to production data."
  type="article"
  publishedDate="2026-06-02"
>
  <div class="container" style="max-width: 720px; padding-top: 3rem; padding-bottom: 5rem;">
    <div class="post-meta" style="font-size:0.85rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">2026-06-02</div>
    <h1 style="font-size: 2.2rem; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.15; letter-spacing: -0.5px; margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
      Introducing gate scan: know your PII exposure before the AI does
    </h1>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 3rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 2rem;">
      gate scan reads your database schema and classifies every column by PII tier —
      so you can configure redaction before you ever connect an AI agent to production data.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-content">
      <p>
        When you connect an AI agent to a production database, you're making a decision about
        what personal information the model can see. Most teams don't make that decision
        deliberately — they find out after the fact, when the model quotes a customer's IRD number
        in a code comment, or includes a Medicare number in a query explanation.
      </p>
      <p>
        gate's job is to intercept that data before it reaches the model. But interception
        works best when you know what you're dealing with up front. That's what
        <code>gate scan</code> is for.
      </p>

      <h2>What gate scan does</h2>
      <p>
        <code>gate scan</code> reads columnar schema JSON from stdin — the output of any
        query against your database's <code>information_schema</code> — and classifies
        every column name by PII tier using the same detection logic gate applies at
        query time.
      </p>
      <p>
        The output is a tiered report: columns grouped by PII category (Government IDs,
        Financial, Health &amp; medical, Contact, and so on), with a summary of total
        exposure across your schema.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here's what it looks like in practice:
      </p>
      <pre>{`$ tkdbr query --sql "SELECT TABLE_NAME, COLUMN_NAME, DATA_TYPE
    FROM information_schema.columns
    WHERE table_schema = 'customers'" | gate scan

gate scan — PII exposure report
════════════════════════════════

 Government IDs (3 columns)
   customers.ird_number
   customers.nhi_number
   employees.tax_file_number

 Financial (5 columns)
   accounts.credit_card_number
   accounts.bank_account
   transactions.account_number
   payments.card_last_four
   billing.iban

 Contact (8 columns)
   customers.email
   customers.phone_mobile
   ...

────────────────────────────────
 16 PII-exposed columns across 6 tables
 Run with --verbose to see all matches`}</pre>

      <h2>Classifying by category, not just flag/no-flag</h2>
      <p>
        Not all PII carries the same regulatory weight. An email address and a NHI number
        both warrant redaction, but they sit in different parts of the NZ Privacy Act and
        the Australian Privacy Principles. A flat "PII / not PII" classification doesn't
        give you enough signal to prioritise your configuration work.
      </p>
      <p>
        gate scan groups columns into 13 Tier 1 categories — Names, Demographics,
        Government IDs, Contact, Date of birth, Location of birth, Address &amp; location,
        Financial, Employment, Health &amp; medical, Online &amp; technical, Biometric,
        and Family &amp; relationships. This maps directly onto the categories regulators
        care about, so you can read the report and immediately know which tables to
        configure forced-column redaction for first.
      </p>

      <h2>Reviewing false positives inline</h2>
      <p>
        Column name heuristics aren't perfect. A column called <code>account_number</code>
        might be an internal product identifier that carries no PII. Running
        <code>gate scan --review</code> lets you walk through every detected column and
        mark false positives to add to the allowlist:
      </p>
      <pre>{`$ ... | gate scan --review

 Financial (5 columns)
   accounts.credit_card_number  ← keep
   accounts.account_number      ← is this a false positive? [y/N]`}</pre>
      <p>
        Columns you mark as false positives are added to the gate allowlist. They'll still
        be checked by Gate 2 value-based patterns (so if an actual credit card number ends
        up in that column, it still gets redacted) — but they won't trigger forced redaction
        on column name alone.
      </p>

      <h2>Why do this before connecting the AI?</h2>
      <p>
        gate's two-layer detection — Gate 1 (column names) and Gate 2 (value patterns) —
        runs at query time. You don't need to pre-configure anything for it to work.
        But there's a class of column that Gate 2 can miss: internal identifiers that
        look like random integers but map to customers in another system. Gate 1's
        forced-column list is the right tool for those, and you can only build that list
        if you know which columns exist.
      </p>
      <p>
        Running <code>gate scan</code> before you start an AI-assisted data project means
        you enter the session with a configuration that's tuned to your schema — not one
        that learns from whatever the model happens to encounter first.
      </p>

      <h2>AU/NZ schemas get extra coverage</h2>
      <p>
        gate scan uses the same pattern library as Gate 2 for column classification,
        which includes AU/NZ-specific categories: IRD numbers, NHI numbers, TFNs, ABNs,
        and Medicare numbers each map to their own Tier 1 category under Government IDs
        and Health &amp; medical. If your schema has columns named <code>ird_number</code>,
        <code>nhi</code>, <code>tfn</code>, or anything similar, they'll surface in the
        scan report with the right regulatory label.
      </p>

      <h2>Try it</h2>
      <pre>{`# Install
cargo install gate-cli

# Run the hook installer
gate init

# Scan your schema
tkdbr query --sql "SELECT TABLE_NAME, COLUMN_NAME, DATA_TYPE
  FROM information_schema.columns
  WHERE table_schema = 'your_schema'" | gate scan`}</pre>
      <p>
        The source is on <a href="https://github.com/GaaraZhu/gate" style="color:var(--accent)">GitHub</a>.
        If you're in a regulated AU/NZ environment and want signed attestation alongside
        the tool, the <a href="/au-nz-pack" style="color:var(--accent)">Compliance Pack</a> is
        where to start.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
